<p>Covers operators, conversions, target-typed literals, copy-and-update, patterns, interpolation, equality, expressions' error handling and <code>except</code>. Rules are compiler-checked; planned features are marked.</p>

<h2 id="operators">Operators</h2>
<p>Precedence, tightest first:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Operators</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>()</code> <code>[]</code> <code>.</code> <code>?.</code></td><td>Call, index, member.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>switch { … }</code></td><td>Tighter than a prefix operator.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>! not - + ~</code> <code>^x</code>, <code>move</code>, casts</td><td>Prefix.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>with { … }</code></td><td>Looser than a prefix operator.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>* / % *% *|</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>+ - +% -% +| -|</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt;&lt; &gt;&gt;</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>.. ..=</code></td><td>Looser than arithmetic and shift, tighter than comparison.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= is in except</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>== !=</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&amp;</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>^</code></td><td>Binary xor.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>|</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>&amp;&amp; and</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>|| or</code></td><td></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>??</code>, <code>catch</code></td><td><code>??</code> is right-associative.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>c ? a : b</code></td><td></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Bayan's own operators:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Operator</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>+% -% *%</code></td><td>Wrapping arithmetic.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>+| -| *|</code></td><td>Saturating arithmetic.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>a..b</code>, <code>a..=b</code></td><td>Range, end excluded or included.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>^n</code></td><td>Index counted from the end.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x in a..=b</code></td><td>Boolean range test.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x ?? y</code></td><td>Fallback when <code>x</code> is null. <code>?? return</code>, <code>?? break</code>, <code>?? continue</code> and <code>?? fail</code> leave early.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>! &amp;&amp; ||</code> or <code>not and or</code></td><td>Boolean operators, two spellings. A project picks one with <code>style.booleans</code>, and <code>bayan format</code> rewrites to it.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<ul>
  <li><code>+ - *</code> panic on overflow in every build mode: <code>panic: arithmetic overflow</code>.</li>
  <li>Integer <code>/</code> and <code>%</code> round toward zero: <code>-7 / 2</code> is <code>-3</code>, <code>-7 % 3</code> is <code>-1</code>.</li>
  <li>Slice ranges may omit an end: <code>xs[..2]</code>, <code>xs[2..]</code>.</li>
  <li>Mixed integers widen; integer plus float is BYN2208.</li>
  <li>Ternary <code>c ? a : b</code> exists, but needs parentheses inside a collection literal.</li>
  <li>No operator definition or overloading.</li>
  <li><code>with</code> binds looser than any prefix operator: <code>move tx with { … }</code> is <code>(move tx) with { … }</code>.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-expressions-operators"/>
<pre><code class="byn">let a: f64 = 2.5
let b: i64 = 2
let c = a + b</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2208:</span> `+` cannot be applied to `f64` and `i64`
  --&gt; x-mixed.byn:3:9

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> let c = a + b
<span class="gut">    │</span>         <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: convert one side explicitly, e.g. `(i64)…`; mixed signedness never converts implicitly</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>BYN2208's help suggests <code>(i64)…</code>, a C-style cast that is not Bayan; write <code>f64(b)</code>.</note>

<h2 id="casts">Casts and conversions</h2>
<p>A conversion is a call on the target type; a C cast <code>(T)x</code> is rejected (BYN1057).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Conversion</th><th scope="col">Rule</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Lossless widening</td><td>Implicit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Narrowing, <code>u8(x)</code></td><td>Explicit. Panics if the value does not fit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>u8.tryFrom(x)</code></td><td><code>u8?</code>, null when it does not fit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Float to integer, <code>i64(2.9)</code></td><td>Drops the fraction.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Text to integer</td><td><code>i64.tryParse(text)</code> gives <code>i64?</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Anything to text</td><td><code>$"{x}"</code> or <code>string(x)</code>. There is no <code>toString()</code> (BYN2031).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>List or array to span</td><td>Implicit when passed as an argument. Elsewhere <code>xs.span</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>User-defined implicit conversions</td><td>Not allowed.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = 5
print(n.toString())</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2031:</span> Bayan has no `toString()`; display is `format()` (derived from
`formatInto(ref StrBuf)`)
  --&gt; x-tostring.byn:2:9

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> print(n.toString())
<span class="gut">    │</span>         <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^ not a Bayan method</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: call `format()`, or interpolate the value: `$"{x}"`
<span class="gut">    │</span> 2 │ print(n.format())
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │         ++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Numeric conversion examples are in <a href="ref-types.html#numbers">Types</a>.</p>
<note>Planned: <code>x.truncate&lt;u8&gt;()</code>, <code>bitcast&lt;T&gt;(x)</code>, <code>checked(expr)</code>, <code>IFrom</code>/<code>ITryFrom</code> for user types, and the <code>Radix</code> forms <code>string(n, as: .hex)</code> and <code>tryParse(text, as: .hex)</code>. Test before relying; <code>$"{n, as: .hex}"</code> gives hex today.</note>

<h2 id="target-typed">Target-typed literals</h2>
<p><code>[…]</code>, <code>["k": v]</code>, <code>{ f: v }</code> and <code>.member</code> take their type from context (variable, parameter or return). No type to take is an error; a <code>.member</code> the type lacks is BYN2003.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Fields use <code>:</code>: <code>Money { cents: 250 }</code>, <code>with { qty: 5 }</code>, <code>take: 10</code>.</li>
  <li>A <code>{</code> starting a statement is a block.</li>
  <li>More than one overload accepting the literal is an error, never a silent pick.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code class="byn">void show(i64 x) { print("i") }
let c: i64 = .red</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2003:</span> `red` is not a member of `i64`
  --&gt; x-ambig.byn:2:15

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> let c: i64 = .red
<span class="gut">    │</span>               <span class="hl">^^^</span></pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="with">Copy-and-update</h2>
<p><code>a with { qty: 5 }</code> copies <code>a</code>, replacing listed fields; the only copy-with-changes form. A type's <code>requires</code> is re-checked on the copy. From outside, a <code>@sealed</code> struct or one with <code>requires</code> changes only this way.</p>
<example name="ref-expressions-with"/>

<h2 id="patterns">Patterns</h2>
<p>A pattern tests a value and may bind parts; it appears after <code>is</code>, in <code>case</code>, <code>for</code> heads and <code>except</code>.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Pattern</th><th scope="col">Matches</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>500</code>, <code>"text"</code>, <code>null</code></td><td>A constant.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>500..=599</code></td><td>A range.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>.high</code></td><td>An enum value or union case.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>.rect(let w, let h)</code>, <code>.circle c</code></td><td>A union case, binding its payload by position or the whole case by name.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>let n</code>, <code>var n</code>, <code>_</code></td><td>Anything, binding it or not. Such an arm covers every case.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>a or b</code>, <code>a and b</code>, <code>not a</code></td><td>Combinations. Patterns use words only.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(3, let w)</code></td><td>A tuple.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>case let n when n &gt; 5</code></td><td>A guard. An arm with a guard never counts toward exhaustiveness.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<ul>
  <li><code>switch</code> over a union, enum or <code>bool</code> must cover every case (BYN2410).</li>
  <li>Type patterns apply only to <code>dyn</code> values and unions.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-expressions-patterns"/>
<note>Planned: list patterns with slices (<code>[var first, .. var rest]</code>, BYN2022), string prefix (<code>"/api/v1/" + var rest</code>), property patterns, <code>switch</code> as an expression with value arms, and named patterns (<code>const inactive = pattern Line { … }</code>). A <code>switch</code> arm is a statement today.</note>

<h2 id="interpolation">Interpolation</h2>
<p><code>$"…{expr}…"</code> writes each hole's value into the string; a format option is a named hole argument.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>as: .hex</code>: lowercase hex, no prefix.</li>
  <li><code>width: 6</code> pads to six characters; <code>fill: '0'</code> chooses the padding character.</li>
  <li><code>digits: 2</code>: a float's digits after the point.</li>
  <li><code>as: .debug</code>: a struct's literal form.</li>
  <li>Floats print the shortest digits that read back to the same value; fixed notation from <code>1e-5</code> to below <code>1e15</code>, exponent outside, lowercase, no <code>+</code>. Whole values drop the point: <code>1.0</code> prints <code>1</code>.</li>
  <li>A distinct type prints as its underlying value, a payload-free union case as its name, an error as its doc-comment summary.</li>
  <li>A struct has no default display (BYN2225): add <code>formatInto</code> or write <code>{x, as: .debug}</code>.</li>
  <li>Lists and maps cannot be interpolated (BYN2225); loop over them.</li>
  <li>Compact <code>{n:hex}</code> and <code>{x:.2}</code> are rejected (BYN1068).</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-expressions-interpolation"/>
<pre><code class="byn">struct P { i64 x }
let p = P { x: 1 }
print($"{p}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2225:</span> `P` cannot be formatted
  --&gt; x-structfmt.byn:3:10

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> print($"{p}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>          <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: add `formatInto(ref StrBuf)` to `P`, or use `{x, as: .debug}`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="equality">Equality</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>==</code> and <code>!=</code> are derived for structs whose fields support them, field by field.</li>
  <li><code>&lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=</code> work on numbers, runes, strings and <code>Symbol</code>s; a bare generic <code>T</code> has no <code>&gt;</code> (BYN2743).</li>
  <li>A distinct type compares only with itself.</li>
  <li>Strings compare byte by byte.</li>
  <li>Null tests with <code>is null</code> / <code>is not null</code>; <code>x == null</code> is BYN2027.</li>
  <li>Comparing a partial struct literal is a lint, since missing fields take defaults; use <code>is</code>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code class="byn">let s: string? = null
if s == null { print("x") }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2027:</span> compare with `is null` / `is not null`, not `==`
  --&gt; x-nullcmp.byn:2:4

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> if s == null { print("x") }
<span class="gut">    │</span>    <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: write `s is null`
<span class="gut">    │</span> 2 │ if s is null { print("x") }
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │      ++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="catch">try and catch</h2>
<p>These operators handle expression failures; error sets and <code>fail</code> are in <a href="errors.html">Errors</a> and <a href="ref-types.html#error-sets">Types</a>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>try f()</code> passes failure to the caller, whose <code>fails</code> clause must list the error.</li>
  <li><strong><code>try</code> covers the whole expression to its right.</strong> Parenthesize the failing part: <code>(try f()) * 2</code>; otherwise BYN1054.</li>
  <li><code>f() catch v</code> gives <code>v</code> on failure.</li>
  <li><code>f() catch switch { … }</code> handles each case and must cover all.</li>
  <li><code>f() catch { … }</code> runs a block; if <code>f</code> returns a value, the block must end in <code>return</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> or a panic (BYN2611).</li>
  <li><code>fail .x</code> leaves with an error; <code>return</code> always returns a value. <code>fail .x if cond</code> and <code>x ?? fail .x</code> follow postfix rules.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-expressions-catch"/>
<pre><code class="byn">union error Bad {
    nan
}
i64 parse(string s) fails Bad =&gt; i64.tryParse(s) ?? fail Bad.nan
i64 twice(string s) fails Bad =&gt; try parse(s) * 2
print("x")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1054:</span> `try` covers `parse(s) * 2`, which cannot fail
  --&gt; x-trymid.byn:5:38

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> i64 twice(string s) fails Bad =&gt; try parse(s) * 2
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                      <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^ cannot fail</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: wrap the part that fails in parentheses: `(try parse(s))`
<span class="gut">    │</span> 5 │ i64 twice(string s) fails Bad =&gt; (try parse(s)) * 2
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │                                  +++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">union error Bad {
    nan
}
i64 parse(string s) fails Bad =&gt; i64.tryParse(s) ?? fail Bad.nan
let n = parse("x") catch { print("bad") }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2611:</span> this `catch` block can complete normally; end it with `return`, `break`, `continue`
or a panic
  --&gt; x-catchfall.byn:5:41

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> let n = parse("x") catch { print("bad") }
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                         <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: leave the block instead of falling through; `parse` returns a value</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: <code>f() catch (Set.variant) value</code>, handling some cases and passing the rest on, is specified but unchecked.</note>

<h2 id="except">except</h2>
<p><code>except</code> subtracts a pattern from a sequence, set or error set, patterns only. A boolean after it is BYN1052; <code>case _ except .draft</code> is BYN1051, fixed to <code>case not .draft</code>. A line starting <code>except</code> continues the previous statement.</p>
<p>On an error set it works today (see <a href="ref-types.html#error-sets">Error sets</a>); not on a sequence.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let xs: List&lt;i64&gt; = [1, 2]
for x in xs except 1 { print("x") }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2022:</span> this expression is not supported in v0.1
  --&gt; x-except.byn:2:10

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> for x in xs except 1 { print("x") }
<span class="gut">    │</span>          <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^</span></pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">for line in order.lines except inactive { … }
let present = users except null</code></pre>
<note>Planned: <code>except</code> on a sequence (BYN2022); use a <code>for</code> loop with <code>continue</code>.</note>
